{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -i \\\"Console\\\\.print|rich\\\\.print|soft_wrap|markup|control\\\" docs/source/console.rst docs/source/introduction.rst\"","aggregated_output":"docs/source/introduction.rst:49:Strings may contain :ref:`console_markup` which can be used to insert color and styles in to the output.\ndocs/source/introduction.rst:51:The following demonstrates both console markup and pretty formatting of Python objects::\ndocs/source/introduction.rst:72:If you would rather not shadow Python's built-in print, you can import ``rich.print`` as ``rprint`` (for example)::\ndocs/source/console.rst:4:For complete control over terminal formatting, Rich offers a :class:`~rich.console.Console` class. Most applications will require a single Console instance, so you may want to create one at the module level or as an attribute of your top-level object. For example, you could add a file called \"console.py\" to your project::\ndocs/source/console.rst:48:To write rich content to the terminal use the :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` method. Rich will convert any object to a string via its (``__str__``) method and perform some simple syntax highlighting. It will also do pretty printing of any containers, such as dicts and lists. If you print a string it will render :ref:`console_markup`. Here are some examples::\ndocs/source/console.rst:50:    console.print([1, 2, 3])\ndocs/source/console.rst:51:    console.print(\"[blue underline]Looks like a link\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:52:    console.print(locals())\ndocs/source/console.rst:53:    console.print(\"FOO\", style=\"white on blue\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:55:You can also use :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` to render objects that support the :ref:`protocol`, which includes Rich's built-in objects such as :class:`~rich.text.Text`, :class:`~rich.table.Table`, and :class:`~rich.syntax.Syntax` -- or other custom objects.\ndocs/source/console.rst:76:The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method will pretty print (format and style) a string containing JSON. Here's a short example::\ndocs/source/console.rst:78:    console.print_json('[false, true, null, \"foo\"]')\ndocs/source/console.rst:97:In additional to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` and :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`, Rich has an :meth:`~rich.console.Console.out` method which provides a lower-level way of writing to the terminal. The out() method converts all the positional arguments to strings and won't pretty print, word wrap, or apply markup to the output, but can apply a basic style and will optionally do highlighting.\ndocs/source/console.rst:152:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style)\ndocs/source/console.rst:153:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style, justify=\"left\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:154:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style, justify=\"center\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:155:    console.print(\"Rich\", style=style, justify=\"right\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:173:You can specify how Rich should handle overflow with the ``overflow`` argument to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` which should be one of the following strings: \"fold\", \"crop\", \"ellipsis\", or \"ignore\". The default is \"fold\" which will put any excess characters on the following line, creating as many new lines as required to fit the text.\ndocs/source/console.rst:190:        console.print(supercali, overflow=overflow, style=\"bold blue\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:191:        console.print()\ndocs/source/console.rst:210:You can also set overflow to \"ignore\" which allows text to run on to the next line. In practice this will look the same as \"crop\" unless you also set ``crop=False`` when calling :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`.\ndocs/source/console.rst:220:    blue_console.print(\"I'm blue. Da ba dee da ba di.\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:226:Rich word wraps text you print by inserting line breaks. You can disable this behavior by setting ``soft_wrap=True`` when calling :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print`. With *soft wrapping* enabled any text that doesn't fit will run on to the following line(s), just like the built-in ``print``.\ndocs/source/console.rst:232:The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` method has a boolean ``crop`` argument. The default value for crop is True which tells Rich to crop any content that would otherwise run on to the next line. You generally don't need to think about cropping, as Rich will resize content to fit within the available width.\ndocs/source/console.rst:235:    Cropping is automatically disabled if you print with ``soft_wrap=True``.\ndocs/source/console.rst:266:You can open the SVG in a web browser. You can also insert it in to a webpage with an ``<img>`` tag or by copying the markup in to your HTML.\ndocs/source/console.rst:321:        console.print(\"[bold red]Hello[/] World\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:329:    console.print(\"[bold red]Hello[/] World\")\ndocs/source/console.rst:344:        console.print(make_test_card())\ndocs/source/console.rst:366:        console.print(locals())\ndocs/source/console.rst:387:                Text.from_markup(f\"[blink]Don't Panic![/blink]\\n{count}\", justify=\"center\"),\ndocs/source/console.rst:404:If Rich detects that it is not writing to a terminal it will strip control codes from the output. If you want to write control codes to a regular file then set ``force_terminal=True`` on the constructor.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
